# hr.view: one horizontal rule, the width of the terminal. Nothing else.
#
# The whole way in, and there is no other:
#
#   @{view:hr}
#
# A decorator with NO payload, which is a shape this engine already knows: it is how
# `@{view:welcome}` is asked for. PUT NOTHING UNDER IT, and the two ways that bites differ:
#
#   - a `---`, or any line that is not a table or a quote, is claimed by no parser, so the
#     decorator engages nothing and BOTH lines print raw. Loud, and self-correcting.
#   - a TABLE or a QUOTE is claimed, handed to this template, and this template reads no
#     field at all, so it is consumed and never drawn. Your table disappears. Nothing here
#     can catch that: "raw over hollow" refuses a render that resolved none of the data it
#     spent substitutions on, and this one spends none.
#
# Hence the rule, and it is the whole contract of this file: the decorator line, alone, with
# a blank line or the end of the message after it.
#
# The rule fills the width because it has nothing to divide. A bare container sizes to its
# CONTENT, and when the rule IS the content there is no body to measure it against, so it
# takes the width it was handed. That is also why the ordinary collapsing does not drop it
# here: a rule alone is not a separator, and the rule that survives only between two lines
# that printed is the one separating two halves of something.
#
# It is drawn in `box_rule`, the furniture grey a box paints its own divisions with, and it
# does NOT spend the tone slot: `@{view:hr, type:warning}` draws exactly this. A rule is
# furniture, it says nothing about the message around it, and a coloured one would claim to.
# That is the same decision lines.view makes, where the kind recolours the labels and never
# the rules between them.
@box bare
@rule
@endbox
